{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nu={\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Two records are added from retained receipts. First, the authority-transition question is closed as a witnessed violation: receipt 7ceae3c2b56c4da6ba40292e01703f4a parses events 14 and 15, where a node that is no longer in the configuration sent TimeoutNow over the transport and the targeted follower became Leader in term 3 while the healthy leader stepped down; the run's exit code is 1 because a different test in the same file failed later, which does not affect that pair of events. Second, the stale-configuration safety account bound to experiment 161fc9455ca947cd80cc76048bf33653-experiment is recorded here with its receipt 1766eefaf1f14339ad1cff36e02a7c33; that receipt's events 9-12 show the acknowledged-write consequence, and an earlier attempt to record this account was not applied to the run state, so the question still carries only its source-derived conditional account.\\\",\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[\n  {\\\"ref\\\":\\\"@k-timeoutnow-authority\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"behavior\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"A server that is no longer part of the configuration can still make a voter campaign and displace the serving leader by sending a TimeoutNow request: the handler stores the transfer trigger without checking the sender, and the resulting campaign carries the leadership-transfer flag that suppresses voters' rejection for nodes that already know a leader.\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"A three-voter configuration from which one server has been removed while it remains transport-reachable; the request is sent over the transport to a surviving follower; the receiving voters know a healthy leader.\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-timeoutnow\\\",\\\"s-requestvote\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"7ceae3c2b56c4da6ba40292e01703f4a\\\":[15]}},\n  {\\\"ref\\\":\\\"@k-stale-quorum-commit\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"behavior\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"A node that leads with a configuration recovered from its snapshot while the configuration in force has more voters computes its commitment quorum from the recovered set: it acknowledged a client entry that only two of the five voters held, and a leader elected afterwards from the configuration in force (five voters, quorum three) did not hold that entry.\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"RestoreCommittedLogs=true with a commit-tracking store; the node restarts from a snapshot recording a three-voter configuration while the configuration in force is five voters and the recommitting entry is in its log but outside the restart scan; leadership of that node is then established and a client write is applied to it.\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-commitment\\\",\\\"s-quorum\\\",\\\"s-scan\\\",\\\"s-restore-committed\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"1766eefaf1f14339ad1cff36e02a7c33\\\":[11,12]}}\n ],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[\n  {\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"f59ffda2c1554082ae054646e92c2508-question-0\\\",\n   \\\"experiment_id\\\":\\\"f59ffda2c1554082ae054646e92c2508-experiment\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-timeoutnow\\\",\\\"s-requestvote\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"7ceae3c2b56c4da6ba40292e01703f4a\\\":[14,15]},\n   \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Current account: the transfer trigger is not restricted to the current leader, and a non-member can therefore displace it. Parsed event 14 records the state before the request: the configuration in force holds two voters, the removed node is not in it, and the target is a Follower in term 2. Parsed event 15 records the outcome after the removed node's TimeoutNow arrived: the target is Leader in term 3, the previous leader is a Follower and old_leader_stepped_down is true, leadership_changed is true, and the request itself returned no error. Both declared comparisons are violated at index 15. Mechanism from source: timeoutNow sets the recipient's leader to empty, sets its state to Candidate and stores candidateFromLeadershipTransfer without inspecting the request's RPCHeader, so any transport-reachable peer can trigger the exchange; the campaign then sets LeadershipTransfer on its vote requests, and requestVote skips its leader-availability rejection when that flag is set, so voters that know a healthy leader still grant. The candidate-membership rule is not bypassed, which is why the trigger works through a legitimate member: the removed node only supplies the trigger. This receipt's exit code is 1 because TestAssurance_StaleConfigCommitQuorum failed later in the same file; the authority pair was parsed from the same run and its sampling is independent of that later failure, since the two events were emitted by a different test before it and the receipt records them with parsed indices 14 and 15.\\\",\n   \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"violated\\\",\n   \\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n   \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n    \\\"The consequence is disruption of a healthy leader rather than loss of committed data: the entry set, terms and logs stay consistent, and the cluster elects a leader again after the term bump.\\\",\n    \\\"Raft tolerates term bumps from any member in general, and a normal campaign from the removed node would be rejected by both the membership rule and the leader-availability rule; what makes the trigger effective is precisely the transfer flag path this account cites.\\\",\n    \\\"The probe used the in-memory transport and delivered the request from the removed node's own transport, which is the situation of a server that was taken out of the configuration but not shut down; a deployment that retires the server entirely would not reach this state.\\\"\n   ],\n   \\\"limitations\\\":[\n    \\\"Measured on one three-voter in-memory cluster with the repository's own test facilities; leader elections are timing-dependent, so which node leads afterwards is not the decisive observation, only that leadership changed.\\\",\n    \\\"The probe does not verify that the removed node's request came from the removed node's own process identity in a networked deployment; the transport carries the sender's header, which the handler ignores.\\\",\n    \\\"No authentication or authorization [REDACTED] outside the transport was modelled.\\\"\n   ]},\n  {\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"a867b593d52841198a36a897ae6a8f0a-question-1\\\",\n   \\\"experiment_id\\\":\\\"161fc9455ca947cd80cc76048bf33653-experiment\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-commitment\\\",\\\"s-quorum\\\",\\\"s-scan\\\",\\\"s-restore-committed\\\",\\\"s-runfollower-gate\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"1766eefaf1f14339ad1cff36e02a7c33\\\":[9,10,11,12]},\n   \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Current account: the configuration gap makes an acknowledged write unsafe, not merely a stale local view. Parsed event 9 records the restarted node holding the snapshot's three-voter configuration (derived quorum two) while the configuration in force has five voters and its log still holds a configuration entry beyond the snapshot; parsed event 10 records that node becoming leader through the library's own transfer API with the same three-voter configuration and a replication set of two peers; parsed event 11 records an Apply returning nil while only two nodes held the payload, below the three acknowledgements the five-voter configuration in force requires, so the assertion that the acknowledged write is backed by a true majority is violated at index 11; parsed event 12 records a leader elected from the five-voter configuration (term four) whose log store has no entry carrying the payload, so the assertion that the acknowledged write survives is violated at index 12. Mechanism from source: the restart applies logs up to the recovered commit index and the scan then starts after that index, so the recommitted configuration entry never reaches processConfigurationLogEntry; the leader's quorum and commitment set come from that recovered configuration, so commitment.recalculate advances the commit index on the (n-1)/2-th match index of the smaller voter set. The reading at index 12 is payload-based rather than index-based: the later leader reports the same last-applied index the acknowledged entry had, but a different entry occupies it. A later re-run of the same harness file failed its transfer phase before reaching these phases, which is why this account cites the earlier receipt that carries them.\\\",\n   \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"violated\\\",\n   \\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n   \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n    \\\"The state needs RestoreCommittedLogs enabled with a commit-tracking store, a snapshot taken while a smaller configuration was committed, and later entries advancing the recovered commit index past the recommitting entry; a cluster that snapshots after every configuration change may not reach it.\\\",\n    \\\"Any later configuration entry or an installed snapshot repairs the recovered configuration, so the window closes once the node receives one; the harness prevents that repair deliberately.\\\",\n    \\\"Both holders of the acknowledged entry were stopped for the final reading, so the observation establishes absence from the new leader rather than permanent erasure if a stopped holder rejoins; the entry was nevertheless one that a true majority had not stored.\\\"\n   ],\n   \\\"limitations\\\":[\n    \\\"Measured on one in-memory five-voter cluster with the repository's in-memory transport, commit-tracking store and mock FSM; production stores and network timing are not covered.\\\",\n    \\\"The harness orchestrates a leadership transfer, a transport disconnection and the shutdown of the two holders, so the ordering is the harness's, though every step uses public APIs.\\\",\n    \\\"A later re-run of the same harness file aborted its transfer phase when the restarted node was already leader, so the account rests on the receipt that completed all phases rather than on repeated runs.\\\"\n   ]}\n ]\n}\njson.dump(u,open('update.json','w'),indent=2); print('written')\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -2\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
